{"reason": "Data processing and feature engineering is the primary bottleneck because the current pipeline relies solely on already-adjudicated violations and static morphological attributes, completely missing active building distress prior to citation. In NYC housing enforcement, HPD inspections leading to Class C violations are overwhelmingly dispatched in direct response to tenant complaints rather than routine audits. Ingesting point-in-time HPD complaint dynamics provides vital leading-indicator signals to detect emerging hazards before formal violations are recorded.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "WHAT: Ingest point-in-time records from lake/full/hpd_complaints strictly prior to each cohort cutoff (T_TRAIN, T_VAL, T_TEST) and engineer temporal distress features per BBL: short-term complaint counts (30-day, 90-day, 365-day), complaint momentum, days elapsed since the latest complaint, complaint intensity per residential unit (complaints_12m / unitsres), and complaint-to-violation conversion ratio (vio_c_count_12m / (complaints_12m + 1)). WHY: The current model achieves high precision on chronic repeat offenders but suffers low recall on newly deteriorating buildings because it lacks upstream tenant complaint visibility before inspection. HOW: Load hpd_complaints projecting only key columns (bbl, receiveddate) with safe_read_parquet, normalize BBLs using normalize_bbl_series, and parse timestamps; inside extract_temporal_features, filter complaints to receiveddate < cutoff, aggregate multi-horizon counts and recency per BBL, merge into df_feat, and compute derivative ratios. Interface constraints: Preserve extract_temporal_features and compute_cohort_target signatures, maintain all existing feature names and definitions, ensure new feature columns are cast to np.float32, and retain the existing train/val/test split and random seeds."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "Data processing and feature engineering is the primary bottleneck because the current pipeline relies solely on already-adjudicated violations and static morphological attributes, completely missing active building distress prior to citation. In NYC housing enforcement, HPD inspections leading to Class C violations are overwhelmingly dispatched in direct response to tenant complaints rather than routine audits. Ingesting point-in-time HPD complaint dynamics provides vital leading-indicator signals to detect emerging hazards before formal violations are recorded.",
  "module": [
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering"
  ],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "WHAT: Ingest point-in-time records from lake/full/hpd_complaints strictly prior to each cohort cutoff (T_TRAIN, T_VAL, T_TEST) and engineer temporal distress features per BBL: short-term complaint counts (30-day, 90-day, 365-day), complaint momentum, days elapsed since the latest complaint, complaint intensity per residential unit (complaints_12m / unitsres), and complaint-to-violation conversion ratio (vio_c_count_12m / (complaints_12m + 1)). WHY: The current model achieves high precision on chronic repeat offenders but suffers low recall on newly deteriorating buildings because it lacks upstream tenant complaint visibility before inspection. HOW: Load hpd_complaints projecting only key columns (bbl, receiveddate) with safe_read_parquet, normalize BBLs using normalize_bbl_series, and parse timestamps; inside extract_temporal_features, filter complaints to receiveddate < cutoff, aggregate multi-horizon counts and recency per BBL, merge into df_feat, and compute derivative ratios. Interface constraints: Preserve extract_temporal_features and compute_cohort_target signatures, maintain all existing feature names and definitions, ensure new feature columns are cast to np.float32, and retain the existing train/val/test split and random seeds."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The baseline reaches an AP of 0.7217 but suffers from two clear bottlenecks: an information bottleneck from omitting acute short-horizon violation velocity and cross-agency distress signals (DOB structural violations and DOHMH rodent inspection failures), and a model mismatch where a lagging Deep Tabular ResNet (val AP 0.6265) underperforms tree-based boosting on sparse tabular count data. By modifying data_processing_and_feature_engineering, model_design, and training_evaluation, we can enrich the feature space with acute 90d/180d acceleration dynamics and multi-agency enforcement signals, replace the ResNet with a complementary depth-wise XGBoost model paired with leaf-wise LightGBM, and optimize a calibrated rank-percentile blend that directly maximizes the official Average Precision metric.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "WHAT: Ingest point-in-time event logs (< cutoff) from dob_violations and dohmh_rodent_inspections, and engineer acute short-term momentum windows (90-day and 180-day prior counts for Class C, Class B, and total violations and complaints), short-term velocity ratios, multi-year chronic recidivism flags, and unit-normalized intensities. WHY: Class C violations are acute physical emergencies (such as heat outages, structural damage, and severe vermin infestations) that flare up rapidly before inspection and correlate strongly with Department of Buildings and health rodent failures, which the baseline's coarse 1-5 year HPD aggregates completely miss. HOW: Standardize BBLs to 10 digits across all lake tables upon loading to prevent merge key collisions, filter DOB and rodent inspection dates strictly prior to cutoff T, aggregate 90d/180d event counts and velocity ratios (90d annualized vs 1y/3y) inside extract_cohort_features, and join onto the cohort. INTERFACE CONSTRAINTS: Preserve extract_cohort_features(entity_df, cutoff_str) signature, maintaining X_train, X_val, X_test, feature_names, and target definitions.", "model_design": "WHAT: Retire the underperforming Deep Tabular ResNet architecture and implement a dual-GBDT model architecture pairing leaf-wise LightGBM with depth-wise XGBoost configured with positive class weighting and regularized tree structures. WHY: High-dimensional, zero-inflated tabular count features are suboptimally learned by tabular neural networks (val AP only 0.6265), whereas combining leaf-wise LightGBM with depth-wise XGBoost (colsample_bytree=0.7, subsample=0.8, max_depth=6) provides architectural split diversity, lowers prediction variance, and directly optimizes ranking precision on skewed positive labels. HOW: Configure lgb.LGBMClassifier with scale_pos_weight=5.0, learning_rate=0.03, num_leaves=31, and instantiate xgb.XGBClassifier with tree_method='hist', scale_pos_weight=5.0, learning_rate=0.03, max_depth=6, subsample=0.8, and colsample_bytree=0.7. INTERFACE CONSTRAINTS: Maintain lgb_model and define xgb_model, ensuring compatible interfaces for fitting on X_tr_mat, y_tr and evaluating on X_val_mat, y_val.", "training_evaluation": "WHAT: Implement a synchronized training and early-stopping evaluation workflow for both LightGBM and XGBoost, rank-normalize validation and test predictions, and optimize the blend weighting strictly to maximize the official Average Precision metric. WHY: Linear score blending can distort model-specific probability calibrations under class imbalance, while percentile rank ensembling preserves relative ordinal rankings and allows direct grid-search optimization for the competition's Average Precision metric. HOW: Fit lgb_model and xgb_model on the 2021 training cohort with early stopping on the 2022 validation cohort, compute fractional rank percentiles via scipy.stats.rankdata, search weights in [0, 1] to maximize average_precision_score(y_val, blended_val), apply the optimal weights to test ranks, and write formatted predictions to ./submission/submission.csv. INTERFACE CONSTRAINTS: Retain submission validation checks (171,587 rows, 10-digit BBLs, no NaNs) and preserve official_val_ap and final_test_preds outputs."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The baseline reaches an AP of 0.7217 but suffers from two clear bottlenecks: an information bottleneck from omitting acute short-horizon violation velocity and cross-agency distress signals (DOB structural violations and DOHMH rodent inspection failures), and a model mismatch where a lagging Deep Tabular ResNet (val AP 0.6265) underperforms tree-based boosting on sparse tabular count data. By modifying data_processing_and_feature_engineering, model_design, and training_evaluation, we can enrich the feature space with acute 90d/180d acceleration dynamics and multi-agency enforcement signals, replace the ResNet with a complementary depth-wise XGBoost model paired with leaf-wise LightGBM, and optimize a calibrated rank-percentile blend that directly maximizes the official Average Precision metric.",
  "module": [
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering",
    "model_design",
    "training_evaluation"
  ],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "WHAT: Ingest point-in-time event logs (< cutoff) from dob_violations and dohmh_rodent_inspections, and engineer acute short-term momentum windows (90-day and 180-day prior counts for Class C, Class B, and total violations and complaints), short-term velocity ratios, multi-year chronic recidivism flags, and unit-normalized intensities. WHY: Class C violations are acute physical emergencies (such as heat outages, structural damage, and severe vermin infestations) that flare up rapidly before inspection and correlate strongly with Department of Buildings and health rodent failures, which the baseline's coarse 1-5 year HPD aggregates completely miss. HOW: Standardize BBLs to 10 digits across all lake tables upon loading to prevent merge key collisions, filter DOB and rodent inspection dates strictly prior to cutoff T, aggregate 90d/180d event counts and velocity ratios (90d annualized vs 1y/3y) inside extract_cohort_features, and join onto the cohort. INTERFACE CONSTRAINTS: Preserve extract_cohort_features(entity_df, cutoff_str) signature, maintaining X_train, X_val, X_test, feature_names, and target definitions.",
    "model_design": "WHAT: Retire the underperforming Deep Tabular ResNet architecture and implement a dual-GBDT model architecture pairing leaf-wise LightGBM with depth-wise XGBoost configured with positive class weighting and regularized tree structures. WHY: High-dimensional, zero-inflated tabular count features are suboptimally learned by tabular neural networks (val AP only 0.6265), whereas combining leaf-wise LightGBM with depth-wise XGBoost (colsample_bytree=0.7, subsample=0.8, max_depth=6) provides architectural split diversity, lowers prediction variance, and directly optimizes ranking precision on skewed positive labels. HOW: Configure lgb.LGBMClassifier with scale_pos_weight=5.0, learning_rate=0.03, num_leaves=31, and instantiate xgb.XGBClassifier with tree_method='hist', scale_pos_weight=5.0, learning_rate=0.03, max_depth=6, subsample=0.8, and colsample_bytree=0.7. INTERFACE CONSTRAINTS: Maintain lgb_model and define xgb_model, ensuring compatible interfaces for fitting on X_tr_mat, y_tr and evaluating on X_val_mat, y_val.",
    "training_evaluation": "WHAT: Implement a synchronized training and early-stopping evaluation workflow for both LightGBM and XGBoost, rank-normalize validation and test predictions, and optimize the blend weighting strictly to maximize the official Average Precision metric. WHY: Linear score blending can distort model-specific probability calibrations under class imbalance, while percentile rank ensembling preserves relative ordinal rankings and allows direct grid-search optimization for the competition's Average Precision metric. HOW: Fit lgb_model and xgb_model on the 2021 training cohort with early stopping on the 2022 validation cohort, compute fractional rank percentiles via scipy.stats.rankdata, search weights in [0, 1] to maximize average_precision_score(y_val, blended_val), apply the optimal weights to test ranks, and write formatted predictions to ./submission/submission.csv. INTERFACE CONSTRAINTS: Retain submission validation checks (171,587 rows, 10-digit BBLs, no NaNs) and preserve official_val_ap and final_test_preds outputs."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The baseline achieves an AP of 0.7303 but suffers from single-period cohort bias and sample sparsity by training exclusively on a single historical cutoff snapshot (T = 2021-01-01), where COVID-19 court freezes and inspection backlogs suppressed Class C enforcement signals. Expanding the training dataset to a multi-year pooled longitudinal panel requires modifying data_processing_and_feature_engineering to construct the point-in-time 2020 cohort and Class C labels and pool it with 2021, and training_evaluation to train the dual GBDT ensemble on the doubled ~346k observation space with early stopping strictly monitored on the 2022 validation cohort. These components are the right places to apply modifications because they eliminate temporal regime bias and double the positive Class C training instances without altering model architectures or leaking future evaluation data.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "WHAT: Construct an additional historical training cohort at cutoff T = 2020-01-01 with a 12-month forward Class C target window (2020-01-01 to 2021-01-01), extract point-in-time features strictly as of 2020-01-01, and concatenate it with the 2021-01-01 cohort into a pooled longitudinal training panel X_train. WHY: Class C violation risk represents chronic structural deterioration and landlord neglect; relying solely on 2021 data restricts positive instances to ~21,550 lots during an anomalous pandemic enforcement period, leading to high false-negative noise and weak tree splits on rare multi-agency distress interactions. HOW: Define df_train_2020 using multiple dwelling lots (unitsres >= 3), assign binary labels from Class C violations in [2020-01-01, 2021-01-01), run extract_cohort_features(df_train_2020, '2020-01-01'), concatenate X_train_2020 and X_train_2021 into X_train, and compute spatial Community District target rates and categorical frequency encodings exclusively on this pooled training dataset before mapping onto X_val and X_test. INTERFACE CONSTRAINTS: Preserve the extract_cohort_features(entity_df, cutoff_str) signature, keep variable names X_train, X_val, X_test, y_tr, y_val, and ensure feature_names and column schemas remain identical across all cohorts.", "training_evaluation": "WHAT: Adapt the LightGBM and XGBoost training and metric-aligned ensembling pipeline to train on the expanded ~346,580 row pooled longitudinal panel (X_tr_mat, y_tr) while maintaining strict early stopping on the unpolluted 2022 validation cohort (X_val_mat, y_val). WHY: Training on pooled multi-year cohorts doubles the training sample size, requiring proactive garbage collection to avoid memory bottlenecks and ensuring that early stopping criteria and rank-percentile blend weights remain strictly optimized against the competition's official validation Average Precision metric. HOW: Feed the pooled X_tr_mat and y_tr into lgb_model.fit and xgb_model.fit with early stopping against (X_val_mat, y_val), perform explicit garbage collection (gc.collect()) prior to evaluation, rank-normalize validation and test predictions via scipy.stats.rankdata, grid search the optimal blend weight w in [0, 1] maximizing average_precision_score, and export test predictions to submission/submission.csv. INTERFACE CONSTRAINTS: Preserve lgb_model, xgb_model, val_preds_lgb, val_preds_xgb, official_val_ap, final_test_preds, and verify submission assertions (171,587 rows, 10-digit zero-padded BBLs, no NaNs)."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The baseline achieves an AP of 0.7303 but suffers from single-period cohort bias and sample sparsity by training exclusively on a single historical cutoff snapshot (T = 2021-01-01), where COVID-19 court freezes and inspection backlogs suppressed Class C enforcement signals. Expanding the training dataset to a multi-year pooled longitudinal panel requires modifying data_processing_and_feature_engineering to construct the point-in-time 2020 cohort and Class C labels and pool it with 2021, and training_evaluation to train the dual GBDT ensemble on the doubled ~346k observation space with early stopping strictly monitored on the 2022 validation cohort. These components are the right places to apply modifications because they eliminate temporal regime bias and double the positive Class C training instances without altering model architectures or leaking future evaluation data.",
  "module": [
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering",
    "training_evaluation"
  ],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "WHAT: Construct an additional historical training cohort at cutoff T = 2020-01-01 with a 12-month forward Class C target window (2020-01-01 to 2021-01-01), extract point-in-time features strictly as of 2020-01-01, and concatenate it with the 2021-01-01 cohort into a pooled longitudinal training panel X_train. WHY: Class C violation risk represents chronic structural deterioration and landlord neglect; relying solely on 2021 data restricts positive instances to ~21,550 lots during an anomalous pandemic enforcement period, leading to high false-negative noise and weak tree splits on rare multi-agency distress interactions. HOW: Define df_train_2020 using multiple dwelling lots (unitsres >= 3), assign binary labels from Class C violations in [2020-01-01, 2021-01-01), run extract_cohort_features(df_train_2020, '2020-01-01'), concatenate X_train_2020 and X_train_2021 into X_train, and compute spatial Community District target rates and categorical frequency encodings exclusively on this pooled training dataset before mapping onto X_val and X_test. INTERFACE CONSTRAINTS: Preserve the extract_cohort_features(entity_df, cutoff_str) signature, keep variable names X_train, X_val, X_test, y_tr, y_val, and ensure feature_names and column schemas remain identical across all cohorts.",
    "training_evaluation": "WHAT: Adapt the LightGBM and XGBoost training and metric-aligned ensembling pipeline to train on the expanded ~346,580 row pooled longitudinal panel (X_tr_mat, y_tr) while maintaining strict early stopping on the unpolluted 2022 validation cohort (X_val_mat, y_val). WHY: Training on pooled multi-year cohorts doubles the training sample size, requiring proactive garbage collection to avoid memory bottlenecks and ensuring that early stopping criteria and rank-percentile blend weights remain strictly optimized against the competition's official validation Average Precision metric. HOW: Feed the pooled X_tr_mat and y_tr into lgb_model.fit and xgb_model.fit with early stopping against (X_val_mat, y_val), perform explicit garbage collection (gc.collect()) prior to evaluation, rank-normalize validation and test predictions via scipy.stats.rankdata, grid search the optimal blend weight w in [0, 1] maximizing average_precision_score, and export test predictions to submission/submission.csv. INTERFACE CONSTRAINTS: Preserve lgb_model, xgb_model, val_preds_lgb, val_preds_xgb, official_val_ap, final_test_preds, and verify submission assertions (171,587 rows, 10-digit zero-padded BBLs, no NaNs)."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The current solution is bottlenecked by single-cohort training on the pandemic-disrupted 2021 snapshot, omits critical cross-agency structural enforcement signals from DOB violations, and pairs LightGBM with an underperforming neural TabNet architecture (0.73459 AP) that yielded negligible ensemble lift (+0.00006). Modifying data_processing_and_feature_engineering enables multi-cohort temporal pooling (adding the 2020-01-01 cohort via PLUTO 19v2 to double training data) and integrates point-in-time DOB safety infractions without BBL key collisions. Modifying model_design and training_evaluation replaces the ineffective TabNet with a regularized depthwise XGBoost classifier, training both tree-based models on the pooled dataset and ensembling their rank-normalized predictions to maximize validation Average Precision.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "WHAT: Expand training data via multi-cohort temporal pooling by extracting an additional historical cohort at cutoff 2020-01-01 from PLUTO 19v2, and augment feature extraction with point-in-time Department of Buildings (DOB) violations (dob_violations) and emergency complaint categorizations. WHY: Single-year training on 2021 is skewed by pandemic inspection backlogs, and HPD data alone misses structural and boiler defects enforced by DOB that precede Class C emergencies; multi-cohort pooling doubles training size to over 340,000 lots while DOB violations supply cross-agency safety signals. HOW: Ingest dob_violations and standardize its 10-digit BBL using normalize_bbl_series, computing point-in-time counts (dob_viol_total, dob_viol_12m, dob_viol_36m, days_since_last_dob_viol) prior to each cutoff. In extract_temporal_features, cleanly merge DOB and morphological features on 'bbl' without column duplication (avoiding the BBL collision bug seen in historical runs), generate both 2020 and 2021 training cohorts, concatenate them into df_train, and compute forward targets consistently for both training windows while preserving extract_temporal_features, normalize_bbl_series, and compute_cohort_target function signatures.", "model_design": "WHAT: Replace the neural GatedResidualTabNet architecture with a regularized, depthwise XGBoost Classifier configuration (get_xgb_model_config) alongside the tuned LightGBM model. WHY: TabNet struggled with zero-inflated tabular counts, yielding poor performance (0.73459 AP) and negligible ensembling diversity, whereas depthwise tree induction in XGBoost complements LightGBM's leaf-wise histogram splits to reduce variance on extreme class imbalance. HOW: Implement get_xgb_model_config returning parameters including tree_method='hist', max_depth=7, learning_rate=0.03, subsample=0.85, colsample_bytree=0.80, scale_pos_weight=2.5, reg_alpha=0.5, reg_lambda=2.0, and random_state=42. Maintain get_lgbm_model_config with aligned hyperparameters to ensure balanced model capacity and diversity across the ensemble.", "training_evaluation": "WHAT: Reconfigure training and evaluation to fit both LightGBM and XGBoost on the pooled multi-cohort dataset, perform out-of-time early stopping on the 2022 validation split, and blend their predictions via rank normalization. WHY: Tree ensembles trained on pooled multi-year cohorts achieve higher generalization without overfitting, and blending distinct tree-growing algorithms (leaf-wise vs depthwise) provides genuine ensemble variance reduction on the official Average Precision metric. HOW: Train lgb_model and xgb_model on X_train and y_train, monitor validation AUC on (X_val, y_val) with early stopping at 50 rounds, compute validation probabilities val_preds_lgb and val_preds_xgb, rank-normalize both to compute val_ensemble = 0.55 * rank_val_lgb + 0.45 * rank_val_xgb, compute final validation AP with average_precision_score(y_val, val_ensemble), and generate submission/submission.csv with normalized test predictions. Interface constraints ensure X_train, y_train, X_val, y_val, X_test, val_ensemble, test_ensemble, and final_score variable names are strictly preserved."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The current solution is bottlenecked by single-cohort training on the pandemic-disrupted 2021 snapshot, omits critical cross-agency structural enforcement signals from DOB violations, and pairs LightGBM with an underperforming neural TabNet architecture (0.73459 AP) that yielded negligible ensemble lift (+0.00006). Modifying data_processing_and_feature_engineering enables multi-cohort temporal pooling (adding the 2020-01-01 cohort via PLUTO 19v2 to double training data) and integrates point-in-time DOB safety infractions without BBL key collisions. Modifying model_design and training_evaluation replaces the ineffective TabNet with a regularized depthwise XGBoost classifier, training both tree-based models on the pooled dataset and ensembling their rank-normalized predictions to maximize validation Average Precision.",
  "module": [
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering",
    "model_design",
    "training_evaluation"
  ],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "WHAT: Expand training data via multi-cohort temporal pooling by extracting an additional historical cohort at cutoff 2020-01-01 from PLUTO 19v2, and augment feature extraction with point-in-time Department of Buildings (DOB) violations (dob_violations) and emergency complaint categorizations. WHY: Single-year training on 2021 is skewed by pandemic inspection backlogs, and HPD data alone misses structural and boiler defects enforced by DOB that precede Class C emergencies; multi-cohort pooling doubles training size to over 340,000 lots while DOB violations supply cross-agency safety signals. HOW: Ingest dob_violations and standardize its 10-digit BBL using normalize_bbl_series, computing point-in-time counts (dob_viol_total, dob_viol_12m, dob_viol_36m, days_since_last_dob_viol) prior to each cutoff. In extract_temporal_features, cleanly merge DOB and morphological features on 'bbl' without column duplication (avoiding the BBL collision bug seen in historical runs), generate both 2020 and 2021 training cohorts, concatenate them into df_train, and compute forward targets consistently for both training windows while preserving extract_temporal_features, normalize_bbl_series, and compute_cohort_target function signatures.",
    "model_design": "WHAT: Replace the neural GatedResidualTabNet architecture with a regularized, depthwise XGBoost Classifier configuration (get_xgb_model_config) alongside the tuned LightGBM model. WHY: TabNet struggled with zero-inflated tabular counts, yielding poor performance (0.73459 AP) and negligible ensembling diversity, whereas depthwise tree induction in XGBoost complements LightGBM's leaf-wise histogram splits to reduce variance on extreme class imbalance. HOW: Implement get_xgb_model_config returning parameters including tree_method='hist', max_depth=7, learning_rate=0.03, subsample=0.85, colsample_bytree=0.80, scale_pos_weight=2.5, reg_alpha=0.5, reg_lambda=2.0, and random_state=42. Maintain get_lgbm_model_config with aligned hyperparameters to ensure balanced model capacity and diversity across the ensemble.",
    "training_evaluation": "WHAT: Reconfigure training and evaluation to fit both LightGBM and XGBoost on the pooled multi-cohort dataset, perform out-of-time early stopping on the 2022 validation split, and blend their predictions via rank normalization. WHY: Tree ensembles trained on pooled multi-year cohorts achieve higher generalization without overfitting, and blending distinct tree-growing algorithms (leaf-wise vs depthwise) provides genuine ensemble variance reduction on the official Average Precision metric. HOW: Train lgb_model and xgb_model on X_train and y_train, monitor validation AUC on (X_val, y_val) with early stopping at 50 rounds, compute validation probabilities val_preds_lgb and val_preds_xgb, rank-normalize both to compute val_ensemble = 0.55 * rank_val_lgb + 0.45 * rank_val_xgb, compute final validation AP with average_precision_score(y_val, val_ensemble), and generate submission/submission.csv with normalized test predictions. Interface constraints ensure X_train, y_train, X_val, y_val, X_test, val_ensemble, test_ensemble, and final_score variable names are strictly preserved."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The current pipeline reaches 0.7321 AP but is constrained by an information bottleneck in feature engineering (missing critical emergency heat and hot water interventions, building alteration recency, visit-level inspection cadence, and block-level tenement distress spillovers) and an unbalanced model ensemble where LightGBM was severely constrained and discarded with 0.00 weight. Enriching features in data_processing_and_feature_engineering directly targets the physical drivers of immediately hazardous Class C violations. Simultaneously, refining model_design and training_evaluation by equalizing GBDT tree capacity, removing distorting positive scale weights, and incorporating symmetric decision trees via CatBoost creates genuine architectural diversity that synergistically boosts the official Average Precision ranking.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "WHAT: Ingest point-in-time event records from hpd_hwo_charges, extract PLUTO building alteration and density metrics (builtfar, residfar, yearalter1, yearalter2), compute distinct inspection event counts across 1y/2y/3y windows, and compute tax block-level spatial Class C risk aggregations. WHY: Class C violations are heavily driven by chronic heat and hot water infrastructure failures and unrenovated tenement decay, while raw violation counts conflate single multi-citation visits with chronic multi-visit enforcement and ignore micro-neighborhood distress. HOW: Load hpd_hwo_charges, standardize BBLs, and aggregate pre-cutoff emergency charges and recency; in PLUTO, compute years since alteration (cutoff_year - max(yearalter1, yearalter2)) and FAR ratio (builtfar / (residfar + 0.01)); in df_viol, aggregate unique inspectiondate counts per lot; and compute tax-block historical Class C violation rates strictly prior to each cutoff. INTERFACE CONSTRAINTS: Preserve the extract_cohort_features(entity_df, cutoff_str) signature, outputting clean arrays for X_train, X_val, X_test, and maintain feature_names.json.", "model_design": "WHAT: Remove the distorting scale_pos_weight=5.0 from the gradient boosted models, expand LightGBM capacity to parity with XGBoost (num_leaves=63, max_depth=7, min_child_samples=25, colsample_bytree=0.7), and integrate CatBoostClassifier with symmetric/oblivious decision trees. WHY: Artificial positive weighting distorts the calibrated rank order needed for global Average Precision, and LightGBM's previous restricted capacity (num_leaves=31) led to underperformance and zero ensemble weight, whereas combining leaf-wise GBDT, depth-wise XGBoost, and oblivious CatBoost maximizes architectural split diversity. HOW: Reconfigure lgb.LGBMClassifier with unweighted binary logloss, num_leaves=63, max_depth=7; reconfigure xgb.XGBClassifier with unweighted binary:logistic, max_depth=7, colsample_bytree=0.7; and configure CatBoostClassifier(iterations=1000, learning_rate=0.04, depth=6, eval_metric='Logloss', random_seed=42). INTERFACE CONSTRAINTS: Maintain lgb_model, xgb_model, and define cb_model to fit uniformly on (X_tr_mat, y_tr) and evaluate on (X_val_mat, y_val).", "training_evaluation": "WHAT: Implement a tri-model training pipeline with validation early stopping for LightGBM, XGBoost, and CatBoost, normalize predictions to percentile ranks, and optimize a 3-way ensemble blend directly maximizing validation Average Precision. WHY: Rank ensembling across distinct tree induction paradigms (leaf-wise, depth-wise, oblivious) lowers prediction variance and robustly optimizes the official ranking metric without probability scale mismatch. HOW: Fit lgb_model, xgb_model, and cb_model on the pooled longitudinal training set with 40-round early stopping on the 2022 validation split, convert raw probabilities into rank percentiles via scipy.stats.rankdata, perform grid search over simplex weights [w1, w2, w3] to maximize average_precision_score(y_val, blended_val), and apply optimal weights to test ranks. INTERFACE CONSTRAINTS: Strictly retain official_val_ap, final_test_preds, and export the final 171,587 rows to submission/submission.csv matching test_entities format."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The current pipeline reaches 0.7321 AP but is constrained by an information bottleneck in feature engineering (missing critical emergency heat and hot water interventions, building alteration recency, visit-level inspection cadence, and block-level tenement distress spillovers) and an unbalanced model ensemble where LightGBM was severely constrained and discarded with 0.00 weight. Enriching features in data_processing_and_feature_engineering directly targets the physical drivers of immediately hazardous Class C violations. Simultaneously, refining model_design and training_evaluation by equalizing GBDT tree capacity, removing distorting positive scale weights, and incorporating symmetric decision trees via CatBoost creates genuine architectural diversity that synergistically boosts the official Average Precision ranking.",
  "module": [
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering",
    "model_design",
    "training_evaluation"
